}

#>wonder_brace_slot_reading_capsule_before_pair
?["When a <…> follows the word and a pair follows the <…>, is the <…> a slot or a relation?"]{
 #:depth #!syntax ~#lens(comparison)
 ~#hypothesis: "A pair on the capsule's right arm marks a slot run, and a word marks a relation: beans<food>(kitchen)[mode]{stew} is one expression with four slots, and bagel<scent>coffee stays left<channel>right."
 ~#expect: "Moving that rule changes the reading of only a handful of live lines in either corpus, and none of them is a relation its author meant."
 ~#unblocks: "~[capsule_slot_after_word]; the consumer's slot_goal"
 ~#tension: "<…> after an operation's own slots wraps the whole operation today ($box[measure]{…}<measurement>); the same rule should settle it, but an operation with a body may also be meant as the left arm of a relation."
 !probe{ "walk every tracked .spw in both corpora, archives excluded, and count medial capsules by left and right arm: after an operation's slots with no right arm, before a pair, and word to word" }
 $%[slot.reading, relation.reading, lines.moved]
}

^["commits"]{
 # Named, in order. Planned ~[name]; landed .[name](hash). Names are never reused.
 ~[medial_stays_on_line]: "&[seed] — a medial capsule's right arm must start on the capsule's own line, as the postfix loop already requires of its slots; failing fixtures for one<tag> then two, and x: $a[m]{b}<reg> then y: …, which merge silently today"
 ~[slot_order_fixtures]: "![seed] — failing fixtures: beans<food>(kitchen)[mode]{stew} is one expression; $box[measure]{presence}<measurement> carries a capsule slot; bagel<scent>coffee and a<r>b<s>c keep the medial reading"
 ~[capsule_slot_after_word]: "&[seed] — a <…> after the word whose right arm is a pair opens the slot run instead of a relation (after ?[capsule_before_pair])"
 ~[capsule_slot_after_operation]: "&[seed] — a <…> after an operation's own frame or body fills that operation's capsule slot instead of wrapping it"
 ~[slot_order_kept]: "&[seed] — the Expression node says the order its slots were written in (after ?[order_on_node])"
 ~[preview_keeps_order]: "&[seed] — previewAST and any slot writer print slots in the author's order, not head, frame, body, scope, capsule"
 .[seed_text_reader](3f53adf2): "^seed[line] — read a line's slots in any order and write it back byte for byte, and hash its slots under a seed"
 ~[consumer_reader_parity]: "![seed] — the consumer's readExpressionSlots and seed agree on its 986 route expressions; the consumer can then retire its scanner into seed's export"
}

^["stream"]{
 >>[2026-11-06 17:24] observe — The owner decided on 2026-11-05, answering spw.quest's slot-order question, that each brace pair names its slot wherever it sits; the consumer's reader and its quest host read either order since 9f2e3af4. Seed at dbd138dd reads [] {} () in any order after a word (word{body}[mode], word(scene)[mode]{body} each one expression), and reads <…> as a slot only after a frame, body or scope (expressions.ts, the postfixCapsule guard). A <…> straight after the word is a medial capsule: beans<food>(kitchen)[mode]{stew} parses as two expressions, {chronicles} <of> (narnia) as a relation.
 >>[2026-11-06 17:24] observe — A second gap the consumer did not report: an operation takes its own frame and body, so $box[measure]{presence.flow}<measurement> reads as Capsule/medial with the operation as its left arm and no right arm, while box[measure]{presence.flow}<measurement> reads as one expression with frame, body and capsule. previewAST prints slots as head, frame, body, scope, capsule whatever order they were written in (preview.ts line 161).
 >>[2026-11-06 17:24] observe — probe of wonder_brace_slot_reading_capsule_before_pair, first pass with all tracked files, archives included: workbench 1,338 files, 79 medial capsules, 1 with a scope on its right arm; consumer 1,155 files, 478 medial capsules, 1 with a scope on its right arm, 62 with a frame (mostly ?<…>[reg=stream] lists), and many after an operation's slots. Run before any ~#expect was on record.
 >>[2026-11-06 17:24] surprise — While classifying the medial capsules: a <…> at the end of a line reads the next line's first term as its right arm. one<tag> then two on the next line parses as one relation, and x: $a[m]{b}<reg> then y: $c[m]{d}<reg> merges the second binding into the first, with no error in either. The postfix loop checks the head's line before taking a slot (headLine in expressions.ts); the medial loop calls skipWhitespace and termNode for its right arm without that check. Planned as medial_stays_on_line, first, since it needs no decision.
 >>[2026-11-06 17:24] observe — Size of the line-crossing defect, live .spw files with archives excluded: in the workbench, 10 medial capsules in 7 files take their right arm from a later line, among them the wip template's own header (#>plan_<slug_id> swallows the #:plan line below it) and two ?<…> lists in operational-topography.spw; in the consumer, 347 in 133 files, of 474 medial capsules, mostly a line ending in <…> followed by the next binding (form: … => loader<{…}> then lifetime: …). The consumer's own reader is per line and never saw this; every workbench reader of those files did.
 >>[2026-11-06 17:24] decide — A plan of its own, not a proposal in syntax-hardware-alignment: the owner has already decided the rule, so what is left is execution and a fixture set, not a staged sample.
 >>[2026-11-06 17:24] note — <…> carries at least five readings in the two corpora: a slot, a relation (bagel<scent>coffee), a path tag (~<tag>"path"), a stream list (?<a, b>), and a type parameter authors borrow from code (loader<{(), getLastError}>, collisions<{name, modules[]}> in a consumer's module notes). ?[capsule_before_pair] settles the first two; the type-parameter lines are the ones medial_stays_on_line moves most. Each commit here waits on reading-drift readings_check to list what it changes.
 >>[2026-11-06 18:10] note — ?[capsule_before_pair] and the order question are held as owner trials before any decision: interpretation-trials wonder_trial_angle_readings, wonder_trial_slot_order and wonder_trial_line_reach, where factshift.center's readings (a concept written first frames the reading below; slots stack in written order) sit beside seed's. medial_stays_on_line still needs no trial.
 >>[2026-11-06 18:10] implement — A text slot reader now lives in seed (packages/spw-seed/src/line: readLine, writeLine, readAt, writeAt, with SLOT_NAMES and BONDS): pairs after the word in any order, once each; a scene or concept may lead; <…> followed by a word is left as a relation; a line stops at its newline; what is not read is kept in rest. 33 fixtures, and laws over each: a line comes back byte for byte, a one-slot change touches only that slot, rest is never dropped. It is the reading consumer_reader_parity will hold the parser to. Joins are read only when a mark is spaced alike on both sides, provisional until wonder_trial_joins is run.
 >>[2026-11-06 18:10] observe — A canvas host now reads and rewrites lines through this reader (its app commit e6ed1b9, carrying the reader build as prerelease 2026.11.07-pre.1 cut from a clean worktree at 3f53adf2). In the browser, beans<food>(kitchen)[mode]{stew} stacks word, concept, scene, body where the host used to keep only beans<food>; bagel<scent>coffee shows its unread tail; a rename on the lines that lost text now changes only the renamed slot.
 >>[2026-11-06 20:20] note — Two slot readers still serve the consumer: seed's (its canvas host) and its own expression-query.js (spw.quest and its manifest), which drops the word in <person>home, ignores nesting in a body and joins only spaced marks. line-kit slot_record_view gives seed's reading under the consumer's slot names, so consumer_reader_parity can run over its 990 route expressions and its reader can become a view.
 >>[2026-11-06 20:28] decide — The owner, 2026-11-06: a mode and a body may lead the word, as a scene and a concept may. line-kit any_pair_leads changes the text reader; for consumer_reader_parity the parser must then read [mode]home and {body}home as one expression with a leading slot, beside <atlas>home, which it reads today as two expressions.
 >>[2026-11-12 09:19] observe — For ~[medial_stays_on_line], reproduced 2026-11-12 on today's parser: a line-final capsule takes the next line's head. a: 48<hours> with b: 2 on the next line reads b inside the capsule, and one<tag>, a blank line, then two reads two into the capsule across the blank line; a comma after the capsule stops both. Both forms belong in the failing fixture. The reading-drift gate (uncommitted, spw fingerprint --readings) cannot yet vouch for this plan: a reading carries kind, structure, word, parent and previous sibling, so a change that only renames the slot a pair fills, with kind, parent and order unchanged, reads as no drift. reading-drift's card names the slot column as its next move.
}

